  "text": "**Solution 2** is the only one whose reasoning holds up, though its conclusion depends on how the problem is read.\n\n- **Solution 3** claims $S=\\{2,3\\}$, which is false. $\\mathbb{CP}^1 = SU(2)/U(1)$ has a single isotropy weight, so its maximal independence is 1. Also, $\\mathbb{CP}^n = SU(n+1)/U(n)$ is simply connected and of maximal rank, and its $n$ isotropy weights $e_i - e_{n+1}$ are linearly independent, so its maximal independence is $n$. Those values are not confined to $\\{2,3\\}$.\n- **Solution 1** gives $n+5$, which is not a number and relies on an unjustified claim that $S=\\{2,3,n\\}$. It also omits the value 1 for $n=1$ and mishandles small $n$.\n- **Solution 2** correctly observes that $\\mathbb{CP}^n$ realises maximal independence $n$ for every $n\\ge 1$, so $S$ contains all positive integers and the sum diverges. I could not verify any source stating the intended finite answer. A reading that gives a finite sum, such as $\\{1,2,3\\}$, would contradict the $\\mathbb{CP}^n$ examples, so I take $\\infty$ as the mathematically defensible answer.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
